AWS Lambda Integration with S3 and CloudWatch

Overview : Terraform project that creates an AWS Lambda image-processing workflow using:

Python 3.14 Lambda
S3 upload and processed buckets
S3 → Lambda trigger
Pillow for JPEG → PNG conversion
CloudWatch logging
IAM execution role and policies
Terraform-managed ZIP deployment

Architecture:

                  Terraform
                      │
       ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
       ▼              ▼              ▼
    IAM Role       S3 Buckets     CloudWatch
                      │
                Upload Bucket
                      │
                  JPEG upload
                      ▼
                Lambda Function
                image_processor
                      │
                 JPEG → PNG
                      │
                      ▼
              Processed Bucket
                      │
                    PNG

IAM :

Lambda uses a single execution role with permissions to:

Read objects from the upload bucket
Write objects to the processed bucket
Write execution logs to CloudWatch

S3 is granted permission to invoke the Lambda function.

Lambda Processing :
JPEG is uploaded to the upload bucket.
S3 triggers the Lambda function.
Lambda downloads the image.
Pillow converts JPEG to PNG.
PNG is uploaded to the processed bucket.
Execution details are logged in CloudWatch.
Terraform

The Lambda deployment package is automatically created using archive_file:

data "archive_file" "example" {
  type        = "zip"
  source_dir  = "${path.module}/lambda"
  output_path = "${path.module}/lambda.zip"
}

Lambda is configured with an increased timeout to support image processing.

Lessons Learned:

Pillow Packaging: Native libraries must be packaged in a format compatible with the Lambda Linux environment.
Terraform ZIP Management: archive_file automatically rebuilds the Lambda ZIP when files in the source directory change.
Lambda Timeout: The default 3-second timeout was insufficient for image processing; increasing the timeout allowed conversion and upload to complete.
Memory Matters: Large images can exceed the default 128 MB memory limit during processing.
CloudWatch Troubleshooting: Adding print() statements helped identify exactly where Lambda execution was stopping.
AWS Signature Error: InvalidSignatureException was caused by system time synchronization issues, not the Lambda configuration.
